---
/**
 * Portfolio one: the whole world in one fund, held well. Every number that describes
 * what the portfolio did is read from the series the research emitter wrote; the
 * one-fund series is a 65/35 US and international index mix standing in for VT, which
 * is also the plain world stock index every other page is compared with.
 */
import Callout from "~/components/Callout.astro";
import Donut from "~/components/Donut.astro";
import DrawdownChart from "~/components/DrawdownChart.astro";
import Figure from "~/components/Figure.astro";
import GrowthChart from "~/components/GrowthChart.astro";
import Hero from "~/components/Hero.astro";
import StatGrid from "~/components/StatGrid.astro";
import { portfolio } from "~/content/portfolios";
import { formatDollars, formatMonths, formatPct } from "~/lib/charts";
import { fundColour } from "~/lib/palette";
import { seriesFor } from "~/lib/series";
import Base from "~/layouts/Base.astro";

const p = portfolio("one-fund");

const oneFund = seriesFor("one-fund");
const sixtyForty = seriesFor("sixty-forty");
const longRun = seriesFor("market", "1929");

const s = oneFund.summary;
const fall = s.worstFall;
const b = sixtyForty.summary;
const long = longRun.summary;

/** "2007-10" as "October 2007". */
function monthName(date: string): string {
  const [year, month] = date.split("-").map(Number);
  return new Date(year ?? 1970, (month ?? 1) - 1, 1).toLocaleString("en-US", { month: "long", year: "numeric" });
}

const dollars = (value: number) => formatDollars(value, "label");
const pct = (value: number, decimals = 1) => formatPct(value, decimals);
const year = (key: string) => s.calendarYears[key] ?? Number.NaN;

const STATS = [
  { value: dollars(s.final), label: "$10,000 became", note: "November 1990 to May 2026, before tax" },
  { value: pct(s.cagrPct), label: "Grew a year", note: "After fund fees" },
  {
    value: pct(fall.pct),
    unit: dollars(fall.dollarsAtTrough),
    label: "Worst fall",
    note: `${monthName(fall.peak)} to ${monthName(fall.trough)}; back to even in ${formatMonths(fall.monthsToRecover)}`,
  },
  { value: p.feeOn10k, label: "Costs a year on $10,000", note: `${p.fee} fee` },
];

const COLUMNS = ["Fund", "What it holds", "Share", "Fee on $10,000", "Account"] as const;
const HOLDINGS = [
  {
    ticker: "VT",
    name: "Vanguard Total World Stock ETF",
    holds: "Every listed company in the world",
    share: "100%",
    fee: "$6",
    account: "Any account",
  },
];
---

<Base
  title="One fund, held well"
  xl
  description="The whole world's stock market in a single fund, in its cheapest form, in an account that fits your needs. What $10,000 did since 1990, how far it fell, what it beats, and the four habits that are arithmetic."
>
  <Hero
    eyebrow="Portfolio 1 of 4"
    title="One fund, held well"
    lede="Buy every listed company in the world in one fund, in its cheapest form, in an account that fits your needs, and hold for the long term. This is the portfolio I would point most people to, and the one every other page here is measured against."
  />

  <div class="prose w-full mt-10">
    <h2>What you buy</h2>
  </div>

  <div class="wide mt-4 grid items-start gap-6 md:grid-cols-[minmax(0,1fr)_12rem]">
    <table role="table" class="stack-table w-full border-collapse text-base">
      <caption class="sr-only">The one fund, what it holds, its share, its fee on $10,000 and which account to hold it in</caption>
      <thead role="rowgroup">
        <tr role="row" class="border-b border-rule-strong text-left align-bottom">
          {COLUMNS.map((c) => <th role="columnheader" scope="col" class="label py-2 pr-3 font-semibold">{c}</th>)}
        </tr>
      </thead>
      <tbody role="rowgroup" class="text-ink-2">
        {
          HOLDINGS.map((h) => (
            <tr role="row" class="border-b border-rule align-top last:border-0">
              <th role="rowheader" scope="row" class="py-2 pr-3 text-left font-medium text-ink">
                <span class="inline-flex items-baseline gap-2">
                  <span class="donut-swatch" style={`background:${fundColour(h.ticker)}`} aria-hidden="true" />
                  <span data-numeric>{h.ticker}</span>
                </span>
                <span class="block text-sm font-normal text-ink-2">{h.name}</span>
              </th>
              <td role="cell" data-label={COLUMNS[1]} class="py-2 pr-3">{h.holds}</td>
              <td role="cell" data-label={COLUMNS[2]} data-numeric class="py-2 pr-3">{h.share}</td>
              <td role="cell" data-label={COLUMNS[3]} data-numeric class="py-2 pr-3">{h.fee}</td>
              <td role="cell" data-label={COLUMNS[4]} class="py-2">{h.account}</td>
            </tr>
          ))
        }
      </tbody>
    </table>
    <Donut
      holdings={[{ ticker: "VT", weight: 100, label: "Every listed company in the world" }]}
      center={{ value: p.fee, label: "a year" }}
      legend={false}
      name="One fund"
      class="justify-center md:justify-end"
    />
  </div>

  <div class="prose w-full mt-6">
    <p>One fund, so which fund goes in which account is never a question. Roughly three fifths of VT is the US
    market; hold it beside a US fund such as VTI and you own the US twice. If you would rather set the split
    yourself, VTI at 0.03% and VXUS at 0.05% do the same job for half the fee.</p>
  </div>

  <div class="prose w-full mt-14">
    <h2>What $10,000 did</h2>
  </div>

  <StatGrid items={STATS} class="wide mt-4" />

  <GrowthChart series={[oneFund, sixtyForty]} highlight="one-fund" class="wide mt-8" title="What $10,000 became">
    One fund against a plain 60/40 mix (60% stocks, 40% US government bonds), 1990 to 2026. Simulated from index
    data, not fund records: a 65/35 mix of US and international index funds stands in for VT, and that mix is also
    the plain world stock index the other portfolios are compared with.
  </GrowthChart>

  <div class="prose w-full mt-6">
    <p>The 60/40 mix became {dollars(b.final)}. Best calendar year: {s.bestYear?.year}, up
    {pct(s.bestYear?.pct ?? Number.NaN)}. Worst: {s.worstYear?.year}, down {pct(Math.abs(s.worstYear?.pct ?? Number.NaN))}.
    In 2020 it made {pct(year("2020"))}; in 2022 it lost {pct(Math.abs(year("2022")))}.</p>
  </div>

  <div class="prose w-full mt-14">
    <h2>The worst fall</h2>
  </div>

  <DrawdownChart series={[oneFund, sixtyForty]} highlight="one-fund" worst={fall} class="wide mt-4" title="Falls from the last high">
    How far below its last high $10,000 sat, month by month, against a plain 60/40 mix. Zero is the last high;
    every dip is a fall from it.
  </DrawdownChart>

  <div class="prose w-full mt-6">
    <p>It was back above $10,000 in {monthName(fall.recovered ?? fall.trough)}, {formatMonths(fall.monthsToRecover)}
    after the {monthName(fall.peak)} peak. The 60/40 mix fell to {dollars(b.worstFall.dollarsAtTrough)} over the same
    months and was back in {formatMonths(b.worstFall.monthsToRecover)}.</p>
  </div>

  <div class="prose w-full mt-14">
    <h2>What it beat, and by how much</h2>

    <p>Almost nothing beats a cheap world index fund reliably. Against it, the whole edge this site's research could
    find is about a coin flip: a 54% chance of being ahead after thirty years. Professional managers deliver the
    exposure they advertise, not extra skill: of 327 tests for extra return, five survived, all five negative.</p>

    <p>What this portfolio beats is the fund you would otherwise own. The average active fund charges 0.57% a year
    and the average index fund 0.09%, so moving saves about <Figure id="fee-gap" inline showUnit={false} /> a year:
    $49 a year on every $10,000, range $40 to $59, without a forecast. If you already hold index funds
    the saving is zero.</p>
  </div>

  <div class="prose w-full mt-14">
    <h2>Who it is for</h2>

    <p>Anyone. It is the default. It suits someone who wants one thing to buy and
    never think about, and who can sit through a fall of about half without selling.</p>
  </div>

  <div class="prose w-full mt-14">
    <h2>How to hold it</h2>
  </div>

  <div class="measure">
    <Callout kind="do-this" class="mt-4">
      <p>Buy VT. Add money when you have it, in whatever account you have. Do nothing when it falls. There is nothing
      to rebalance. Before your first purchase, check your broker's list of ETFs that carry a purchase fee. The
      details are on <a href="/how-to-hold/">how to hold it</a>.</p>
    </Callout>
  </div>

  <div class="prose w-full mt-14">
    <h2>The four habits</h2>

    <p>What you keep depends on costs, taxes and decisions you can repeat.</p>

    <p><strong>Compare total costs.</strong> Start with broad funds that provide the exposure you want.
    Fees after lending, trading spreads and broker charges all matter. A cheaper fee does not make two
    different portfolios equivalent. The <a href="/funds/">fund list</a> separates these costs.</p>

    <p><strong>Use the accounts you actually have.</strong> Check contribution and withdrawal rules,
    tax treatment and available investments. With one fund there is no ranking of funds to place, but the
    choice between taxable, pre-tax and Roth accounts can still matter.</p>

    <p><strong>Check distributions and structure.</strong> An ETF can reduce taxable capital-gain payouts,
    but the label does not guarantee tax efficiency. Compare the actual fund's distributions and costs
    with alternatives available in your account.</p>

    <p><strong>Avoid unnecessary sales.</strong> Deferring a taxable gain can leave more money invested.
    Selling may still be appropriate for spending, a changed allocation or a better investment after costs.
    Compare the tax bill with the reason for the change.</p>
  </div>

  <div class="prose w-full mt-14">
    <h2>What could go wrong</h2>

    <p>In the ten years to February 2009 US stocks returned <Figure id="flat-decade" inline />, so $10,000 put in at
    the start of that decade was worth about $7,700 at the end. Today the seven largest US companies are 34% of the
    S&amp;P 500 (July 2026), and US stocks cost about 41 years of average profits, a level seen in only 19 of 1,748
    months since 1881, almost all of them in 1999 and 2000. That price has
    predicted deeper falls, not lower returns. Index-fund investors as a group earned about 1.1% a year less than
    the funds they held.</p>
  </div>

  <div class="prose w-full mt-14">
    <h2>Should you stop here</h2>

    <p>Most people should. <a href="/portfolios/value-lean/">Portfolio 2</a> leans a third of the money toward
    cheaper companies. <a href="/portfolios/with-trend/">Portfolio 3</a> adds a trend-following fund, the one
    holding here that can rise when stocks fall. <a href="/portfolios/cautious/">Portfolio 4</a> puts half in
    inflation-protected government bonds, for someone who would sell after a fall of about 40%.</p>
  </div>

  <div class="prose w-full mt-14">
    <h2>The long history</h2>

    <p>Simulated, from US stock index data. Over 1929 to 2025 a US all-stock index grew {pct(long.cagrPct)} a year,
    and $10,000 at the {monthName(long.worstFall.peak)} peak fell to {dollars(long.worstFall.dollarsAtTrough)} by
    {monthName(long.worstFall.trough)}, a fall of {pct(Math.abs(long.worstFall.pct))}. It was not back above $10,000
    until {monthName(long.worstFall.recovered ?? long.worstFall.trough)}, {formatMonths(long.worstFall.monthsToRecover)}
    later. That history is US stocks only, and nobody who lived it knew how it would end.</p>
  </div>
</Base>
